.TH c "" "Command"
.SH NAME
c \- multi-column output
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBc [\-w\fIN\fB] [\-l\fIN\fB] [\-012]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
c -- multi-column output
Usage:	c [ -wN ] [ -012 ]
	-wN	Set the page width to N columns
	-0	Order fields horizontally across the page
	-1	Order fields vertically down each column (default)
	-2	Special case of -1
.HE
.B c
reads lines from the standard input and prints them in columns on
the standard output, using as many columns as fit across the page
given the width of the longest input line.
.PP
.B c
recognizes the following options:
.IP \fB\-w\fIN\fR
Set the width of the page to
.I N
columns (default: 80).
.IP \fB\-l\fIN\fR
Set the length of the page to
.I N
lines.
.IP \fB\-0\fR
Order the fields horizontally across the page.
.IP \fB\-1\fR
Order the fields vertically down each column (the default).
.IP \fB\-2\fR
A special case of
.BR \-1 .
.PP
Options may also be supplied in the environment variable
.BR C ,
separated by white space; options given on the command line are
processed afterward and so override those taken from the
environment.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B pr
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B c
prints "out of mem" and exits with status one if it cannot allocate
enough memory to hold its input.
